-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP72687864007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-2000x1629.jpg 2000w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP72687864007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-780x635.jpg 780w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP72687864007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-400x326.jpg 400w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP72687864007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-706x575.jpg 706w" data-sizes="(max-width: 1200px) 100vw, 1200px" data-attachment-id="297372" data-permalink="https://wausaupilotandreview.com/2026/06/25/nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went/bobby-portis-holds-the-larry-obrien-trophy-as-part-of-the-celebration-of-the-milwaukee-bucks-2021-nba-championship/" data-orig-file="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP72687864007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK.jpg" data-orig-size="2560,2085" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="Bobby Portis holds the Larry O&amp;#8217;Brien Trophy as part of the celebration of the Milwaukee Bucks 2021 NBA championship." data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;Bobby Portis holds the Larry O&amp;#8217;Brien Trophy as part of the celebration of the Milwaukee Bucks 2021 NBA championship.&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP72687864007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-771x628.jpg" /><figcaption><span>Bobby Portis holds the Larry O'Brien Trophy as part of the celebration of the Milwaukee Bucks 2021 NBA championship.</span></figcaption></figure><div class="main-content"><article id="post-297368" class="post-297368 post type-post status-publish format-standard has-post-thumbnail hentry category-afternoon-update category-milwaukee-bucks category-sports category-top-stories entry"><div class="entry-content"><div style='margin:5px auto; margin-bottom: 15px;' id='zone_load_1253468965'><broadstreet-zone callback="zone_load_1253468965" zone-id="58241" keywords="not_home_page,not_landing_page,is_article_page,afternoon-update,milwaukee-bucks,sports,sports,top-stories,nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went,post" soft-keywords="true" zone-alias=""></broadstreet-zone></div><script data-cfasync="false" src="/cdn-cgi/scripts/5c5dd728/cloudflare-static/email-decode.min.js"></script><script src="data:text/javascript;base64,d2luZG93LnpvbmVfbG9hZF8xMjUzNDY4OTY1PWZ1bmN0aW9uKHosZCl7aWYoIWQuY291bnQpZG9jdW1lbnQuZ2V0RWxlbWVudEJ5SWQoJ3pvbmVfbG9hZF8xMjUzNDY4OTY1Jykuc3R5bGUuZGlzcGxheT0nbm9uZSd9" defer></script><div class="wp-block-custom-everlit-iframe-embed"><iframe data-lazyloaded="1" src="about:blank" title="Everlit Audio Player" data-src="https://everlit.audio/embeds/artl_NKDzNiR2bJP?ui_title_intro=Hear+this+story%3A&amp;ui_title_icon=headphones&amp;client=wp&amp;client_version=3.3.3" width="100%" height="136px" frameborder="0" allow="accelerometer; autoplay; clipboard-write; encrypted-media; gyroscope; picture-in-picture; web-share" allowfullscreen></iframe></div><p class="wp-block-paragraph">USA TODAY NETWORK<p class="wp-block-paragraph">uly 20 will mark the five-year anniversary of the Milwaukee Bucks winning the NBA championship. Thanks to a Herculean performance from superstar Giannis Antetokounmpo, Milwaukee defeated the Phoenix Suns in six games and landed its first championship in 50 years.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With the trade of Antetokounmpo and Bobby Portis to the Miami Heat, there are no longer any players left on the Milwaukee roster who contributed to that unforgettable run.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Do you remember all the players on the 2021 championship roster? Here&#8217;s a refresher:</p><div style='margin:5px auto; margin-bottom: 15px;' id='zone_load_1173707031'><broadstreet-zone place="0" callback="zone_load_1173707031" zone-id="58242" keywords="not_home_page,not_landing_page,is_article_page,afternoon-update,milwaukee-bucks,sports,sports,top-stories,nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went,post" soft-keywords="true" zone-alias=""></broadstreet-zone></div><script src="data:text/javascript;base64,d2luZG93LnpvbmVfbG9hZF8xMTczNzA3MDMxPWZ1bmN0aW9uKHosZCl7aWYoIWQuY291bnQpZG9jdW1lbnQuZ2V0RWxlbWVudEJ5SWQoJ3pvbmVfbG9hZF8xMTczNzA3MDMxJykuc3R5bGUuZGlzcGxheT0nbm9uZSd9" defer></script> <div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="alignright size-large is-resized"><img data-lazyloaded="1" src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciIHdpZHRoPSI3NzEiIGhlaWdodD0iOTY0IiB2aWV3Qm94PSIwIDAgNzcxIDk2NCI+PHJlY3Qgd2lkdGg9IjEwMCUiIGhlaWdodD0iMTAwJSIgc3R5bGU9ImZpbGw6I2NmZDRkYjtmaWxsLW9wYWNpdHk6IDAuMTsiLz48L3N2Zz4=" decoding="async" width="771" height="964" data-attachment-id="297371" data-permalink="https://wausaupilotandreview.com/2026/06/25/nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went/milwaukee-bucks-forward-giannis-antetokounmpo-hugs-phoenix-suns-coach-mike-budenholzer/" data-orig-file="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK.jpg" data-orig-size="2048,2560" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="Milwaukee Bucks forward Giannis Antetokounmpo hugs Phoenix Suns coach Mike Budenholzer." data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;Milwaukee Bucks forward Giannis Antetokounmpo hugs Phoenix Suns coach Mike Budenholzer.&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-771x964.jpg" data-src="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-771x964.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-297371" style="aspect-ratio:0.7997859541250214;width:459px;height:auto" data-srcset="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-771x964.jpg 771w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-336x420.jpg 336w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-768x960.jpg 768w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1229x1536.jpg 1229w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1638x2048.jpg 1638w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1200x1500.jpg 1200w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-819x1024.jpg 819w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-2000x2500.jpg 2000w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-780x975.jpg 780w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-400x500.jpg 400w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-706x883.jpg 706w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP82769086007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK.jpg 2048w" data-sizes="(max-width: 771px) 100vw, 771px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Milwaukee Bucks forward Giannis Antetokounmpo hugs Phoenix Suns coach Mike Budenholzer.</figcaption></figure></div><p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Giannis Antetokounmpo</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 26</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Drafted 15th in 2013</li><li>Where he is now: On his way to the Miami Heat</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">Antetokounmpo scored 30-plus points in six of the seven games during an epic Eastern Conference semifinal series against Brooklyn Nets, including 40 points in the Game 7 that went to overtime. He went down with what looked like a catastrophic knee injury during the Eastern Conference finals against the Atlanta Hawks, only to resurrect for an unreal NBA Finals performance against the Suns. He posted 20 points and 17 rebounds in his first game back, then 42 and 12 in the second. With the Bucks down in the series 0-2, Antetokounmpo scored 41 in Game 3, finished two assists shy of a triple-double in Game 4 and authored an unforgettable block to seal the win, then threw down a monumental alley-oop dunk to seal Game 5 in Phoenix. In Game 6, he made 17 of 19 free throws and finished with 50 points. The Finals MVP celebrated with Chick-Fil-A.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Khris Middleton</strong></p><div style='margin:5px auto; margin-bottom: 15px;' id='zone_load_994173043'><broadstreet-zone place="1" callback="zone_load_994173043" zone-id="58242" keywords="not_home_page,not_landing_page,is_article_page,afternoon-update,milwaukee-bucks,sports,sports,top-stories,nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went,post" soft-keywords="true" zone-alias=""></broadstreet-zone></div><script src="data:text/javascript;base64,d2luZG93LnpvbmVfbG9hZF85OTQxNzMwNDM9ZnVuY3Rpb24oeixkKXtpZighZC5jb3VudClkb2N1bWVudC5nZXRFbGVtZW50QnlJZCgnem9uZV9sb2FkXzk5NDE3MzA0MycpLnN0eWxlLmRpc3BsYXk9J25vbmUnfQ==" defer></script> <ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 29</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Added in 2013 trade with Detroit Pistons</li><li>Where he is now: Dallas Mavericks</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">His No. 22 jersey will hang in the rafters at Fiserv Forum after an excellent 12-year run, and his work in the 2021 postseason certainly will play on the career highlight reel.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With Antetokounmpo sidelined by injury, Middleton took the reins against Atlanta, scoring 26 and 32 points in the final two games of the Eastern Conference finals, both wins. He scored 40 points in Game 4 of the NBA Finals against the Suns, one of six career postseason games with the Bucks in which he scored 35 points or more. He averaged 23.6 points in his 23 playoff contests, with 5.1 assists and 7.6 rebounds. He also hit a buzzer-beating basket in the first postseason game against Miami, setting the tone for a series sweep. Injuries became a nuisance for Middleton thereafter, though he was a standout during the 2024 playoffs against Indiana with Antetokounmpo once again sidelined. Middleton was traded to the Washington Wizards during the 2024-25 season in a deal that brought Kyle Kuzma to Milwaukee, then moved to Dallas in February 2026.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Jrue Holiday</strong></p><div style='margin:5px auto; margin-bottom: 15px;' id='zone_load_504209300'><broadstreet-zone place="2" callback="zone_load_504209300" zone-id="58242" keywords="not_home_page,not_landing_page,is_article_page,afternoon-update,milwaukee-bucks,sports,sports,top-stories,nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went,post" soft-keywords="true" zone-alias=""></broadstreet-zone></div><script src="data:text/javascript;base64,d2luZG93LnpvbmVfbG9hZF81MDQyMDkzMDA9ZnVuY3Rpb24oeixkKXtpZighZC5jb3VudClkb2N1bWVudC5nZXRFbGVtZW50QnlJZCgnem9uZV9sb2FkXzUwNDIwOTMwMCcpLnN0eWxlLmRpc3BsYXk9J25vbmUnfQ==" defer></script> <ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 30</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Added in 2020 four-team trade from the New Orleans Pelicans</li><li>Where he is now: Portland Trail Blazers</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">The missing ingredient for the Bucks proved expensive: The team had to surrender two first-round draft picks and offer up pick swaps on two others to make it happen. But Holiday delivered. An outstanding defender (first-team all-defensive team), he also averaged 17.7 points and 6.1 assists per game, catalyzing Milwaukee&#8217;s playoff run. His steal and alley-oop to Antetokounmpo during the Game 5 Finals win over Phoenix became one of the most iconic moments in Wisconsin sports history.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He averaged 17.3 points in the postseason and stayed two more years, including an all-star season, before the Bucks traded him to Portland in the deal that brought Damian Lillard to Milwaukee. Portland then sent him to Boston, where he helped the Celtics win another title in 2024. Boston then traded him back to the Blazers, where he averaged 16.3 points in 2025-26 at age 35.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Brook Lopez</strong></p><div style='margin:5px auto; margin-bottom: 15px;' id='zone_load_1159496375'><broadstreet-zone place="3" callback="zone_load_1159496375" zone-id="58242" keywords="not_home_page,not_landing_page,is_article_page,afternoon-update,milwaukee-bucks,sports,sports,top-stories,nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went,post" soft-keywords="true" zone-alias=""></broadstreet-zone></div><script src="data:text/javascript;base64,d2luZG93LnpvbmVfbG9hZF8xMTU5NDk2Mzc1PWZ1bmN0aW9uKHosZCl7aWYoIWQuY291bnQpZG9jdW1lbnQuZ2V0RWxlbWVudEJ5SWQoJ3pvbmVfbG9hZF8xMTU5NDk2Mzc1Jykuc3R5bGUuZGlzcGxheT0nbm9uZSd9" defer></script> <ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 32</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in 2018 as a free agent</li><li>Where he is now: Los Angeles Clippers</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lopez reinvented himself before signing with the Bucks, transitioning from strictly an inside presence to a center capable of shooting 3-pointers (but still maintaining his elite defense). The Bucks used that to great effect, and the all-time leading scorer in Nets history left a new mark with the Bucks. He averaged 13.0 points in the postseason with 1.5 blocks and 5.9 rebounds. His career was far from over; he went on to average 19.4 points the following year and spent four more seasons in Milwaukee, including a 2022-23 season in which he finished second in the defensive player of the year voting.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">He signed with the Los Angeles Clippers last offseason after seven yeas with Milwaukee, one of the most deft free-agent signings in franchise lore. He averaged 8.5 points for Los Angeles in 21.9 minutes per game in 2025-26.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>P.J. Tucker</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 35</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Added before the 2021 trade deadline from the Rockets</li><li>Where he is now: Retired</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">Tucker played a mere 20 regular-season games with the Bucks in his career, seeing more action in the playoffs (23 games), but he&#8217;ll be celebrated in Milwaukee forever. A bruising defensive presence, Tucker provided the edge and attitude the team needed at the deadline, even while adding only 4.3 points and 4.8 rebounds in his playoff opportunities. Often tasked with guarding elite opponents, Tucker in some ways became the face of the title run, and Bucks fans were disappointed when Milwaukee elected not to bring him back the following season. He spent stints with multiple teams before officially announcing his retirement in May 2026. He didn&#8217;t play in the NBA in 2025-26.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Bobby Portis</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 25</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in 2020 as a free agent</li><li>Where he is now: On his way to the Miami Heat</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">&#8220;Bob-by! Bob-by!&#8221; With an underdog mentality and workmanlike approach, Portis became a popular player immediately among Bucks fans, coming off the bench to provide a spark. He averaged 8.8 points and 5.0 rebounds during the postseason, a necessary backup center to Lopez who also shot 47% from 3-point range during the regular season. Five years later, he remained a fan fave in the Cream City, and his departure in the Antetokounmpo trade to Miami will be a tough blow for many fans to absorb.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Pat Connaughton</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 28</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in 2018 as a free agent</li><li>Where he is now: Charlotte Hornets</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">Like Portis and Lopez, Connaughton proved to be a shrewd free-agent signing who paid off in far greater ways than the team could have imagined. After three seasons in Portland, Connaughton saw his playing time increase dramatically in Milwaukee, where he came off the bench and shot well (37% in 2021 from 3-point range) and proved to be an excellent rebounding guard (4.8 boards per game that season). He saw action in all 23 playoff games and remained with the Bucks through the 2024-25 season, becoming a popular fixture for fans. He also was involved in Milwaukee real estate. Connaughton was traded to Charlotte before the 2025-26 season and averaged 2.6 points per game.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Bryn Forbes</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 27</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in 2020 as a free agent</li><li>Where he is now: Out of pro basketball</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">Forbes averaged 10 points in his one season with the Bucks, appearing in 70 games and starting 10, with a career-best 45% mark from 3-point range. He chipped in with 6.6 points in 20 playoff games, playing 14 minutes per contest. Forbes signed a free-agent deal with San Antonio after the title season and last saw action in the NBA in 2023 with Minnesota. He was arrested in 2024 on a felony charge of assault (strangulation of a family member).<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Donte DiVincenzo</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 24</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Drafted 17th in 2018</li><li>Where he is now: Minnesota Timberwolves</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">DiVincenzo played only three playoff games, but he certainly would have played more had he not suffered a foot injury against Miami in the opening round. One of the few homegrown draft picks on the roster, DiVincenzo had averaged 13.7 points in his third season, with 7.5 rebounds and 4.0 assists, and he started all 66 regular-season games in which he appeared. He remained with the team the next year but was dealt in February 2022 to the Sacramento Kings in an ill-fated swap that brought Serge Ibaka to the Bucks. He has since gone on playoff runs with the Golden State Warriors, New York Knicks and Timberwolves, winning at least one playoff series each year. However, he did just tear his Achilles in the 2026 playoffs and likely will miss all of the 2026-27 season.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Jeff Teague</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 32</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in April 2021 as a free agent</li><li>Where he is now: Out of pro basketball</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">After getting waived by the Orlando Magic, Teague reunited with his former head coach Mike Budenholzer in Milwaukee and became a sneaky veteran addition in the final year of his NBA career. Teague played sparingly in the postseason but appeared in 16 games as a backup point guard, which included an 11-point effort in Game 6 of the Eastern Conference finals against his former team, the Hawks, that helped the Bucks clinch the series. He later became a scout for the Hawks and now coaches high school basketball at his alma mater in Indianapolis, Pike High School.<div class="wp-block-image"><figure class="alignright size-large is-resized"><img data-lazyloaded="1" src="data:image/svg+xml;base64,PHN2ZyB4bWxucz0iaHR0cDovL3d3dy53My5vcmcvMjAwMC9zdmciIHdpZHRoPSI3NzEiIGhlaWdodD0iNTkwIiB2aWV3Qm94PSIwIDAgNzcxIDU5MCI+PHJlY3Qgd2lkdGg9IjEwMCUiIGhlaWdodD0iMTAwJSIgc3R5bGU9ImZpbGw6I2NmZDRkYjtmaWxsLW9wYWNpdHk6IDAuMTsiLz48L3N2Zz4=" decoding="async" width="771" height="590" data-attachment-id="297370" data-permalink="https://wausaupilotandreview.com/2026/06/25/nobody-is-left-from-the-2021-bucks-title-team-heres-where-they-went/milwaukee-bucks-brothers-giannis-antetokounmpo-and-thanasis-antetokounmpo-show-off-their-championship-rings-during-a-ring-ceremony-2/" data-orig-file="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1.jpg" data-orig-size="2560,1958" data-comments-opened="0" data-image-title="Milwaukee Bucks brothers Giannis Antetokounmpo and Thanasis Antetokounmpo show off their championship rings during a ring ceremony." data-image-description="" data-image-caption="&lt;p&gt;Milwaukee Bucks brothers Giannis Antetokounmpo and Thanasis Antetokounmpo show off their championship rings during a ring ceremony.&lt;/p&gt;
" data-large-file="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-771x590.jpg" data-src="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-771x590.jpg" alt="" class="wp-image-297370" style="aspect-ratio:1.3067980125432324;width:463px;height:auto" data-srcset="https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-771x590.jpg 771w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-336x257.jpg 336w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-768x587.jpg 768w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-1536x1175.jpg 1536w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-2048x1566.jpg 2048w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-1200x918.jpg 1200w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-1024x783.jpg 1024w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-2000x1530.jpg 2000w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-780x597.jpg 780w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-400x306.jpg 400w, https://cdn.wausaupilotandreview.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/2026-06-25T102921Z_307103361_MT1USDAYNETWIP83047899007_FRTPIP_4_USA-TODAY-NETWORK-1-706x540.jpg 706w" data-sizes="(max-width: 771px) 100vw, 771px" /><figcaption class="wp-element-caption">Milwaukee Bucks brothers Giannis Antetokounmpo and Thanasis Antetokounmpo show off their championship rings during a ring ceremony.</figcaption></figure></div><p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Thanasis Antetokounmpo</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 28</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in 2019 as a free agent</li><li>Where he is now: Podcasting</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">The older brother of Giannis and the energy-generating bench option played in 13 playoff games and saw 3.5 minutes of action per contest, but his presence was more than just numbers, allowing the team&#8217;s star to be surrounded by family and generating his own following with his effusive personality. He&#8217;s again a free agent after the 2025-26 season and, presumably, won&#8217;t re-sign with the Bucks. His Thanalysis podcast often welcomes many high-profile guests in the Wisconsin sports realm.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Elijah Bryant</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 25</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in May 2021 as a free agent</li><li>Where he is now: Playing overseas</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">A college player at Elon and BYU, Bryant was signed out of an Israeli league in the final week of the regular season, then scored 16 points against the Chicago Bulls in his one and only NBA regular-season game. But he also appeared in 11 playoff games, even while playing a grand total of 50 minutes and averaging 1.3 points. After that, Bryant returned overseas, playing in Turkey through the 2024-25 season and now in Israel.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Sam Merrill</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 24</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Acquired draft rights in 2020</li><li>Where he is now: Cleveland Cavaliers</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Bucks landed the rights to their own second-round pick in 2020, re-acquiring the asset after a 2019 trade that sent Thon Maker to the Pistons and brought Nikola Miroti? to the Bucks. As part of the deal to get Jrue Holiday, Milwaukee got the pick back. Drafted out of BYU, Merrill saw action in 30 games that year, shooting an impressive 44.7% from 3-point range in limited work. He saw the floor in eight playoff games and was traded to Memphis in the deal that brought Grayson Allen to the Bucks. Merrill has enjoyed recent success in the past four years in Cleveland, and the career 39.7% 3-point shooter signed a four-year deal worth $38 million to remain with the Cavs last offseason. He started 38 games in 2025-26, shot the lights out (42.1% from 3) and scored 12.8 points per game for a team that reached the Eastern Conference finals.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Mamadi Diakite</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 24</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed on two-way contract in 2020</li><li>Where he is now: Playing overseas</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">The rookie out of Virginia played in just 14 regular-season games for the Bucks, but he impressed enough to have his contract converted to a two-year deal in April. The power forward saw the floor in seven playoff games; he was waived by the Bucks before the 2021 season and caught on with Oklahoma City. He last saw brief NBA action in 2023-24 with the Spurs and Knicks and spent the 2025-26 season in Spain.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Jordan Nwora</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 22</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Drafted with 45th pick in 2020</li><li>Where he is now: Playing overseas</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">Drafted out of Louisville, Nwora spent two-plus years with the Bucks, and though he saw the floor sparingly in his rookie season (5.7 points, 9.1 minutes per game), he saw an uptick thereafter. The Bucks traded him in a four-team deal with George Hill and Serge Ibaka that brought Jae Crowder to Milwaukee. Nwora saw the floor in five playoff games and scored 15 total points in the playoff run. He played with Indiana and Toronto in 2023-24 and then in Turkey and now Serbia.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Justin Jackson</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 25</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in April 2021 on a two-way contract</li><li>Where he is now: Out of pro basketball</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">The Bucks added Jackson on a two-way deal late in the season and he appeared in a single regular-season game. He saw action in five playoff games and scored six points overall, then signed elsewhere in the postseason and bounced around until his last NBA appearance in 2023-24. He did finish with 280 NBA games under his belt, mostly played in Dallas and Sacramento before arriving with the Bucks.<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Axel Toupane</strong><ul class="wp-block-list"><li>Age in 2021: 28</li><li>How the Bucks acquired him: Signed in March 2021 on two-way contract</li><li>Where he is now: Playing overseas</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph">The native of France appeared in eight regular-season games and then four playoff games, scoring five points in the postseason. They were his last days in the NBA; he went on to play in the G-League, France, Lebanon, Taiwan and now Portugal.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Did not appear in 2021 postseason for Bucks, but played during 2020-21 season<ul class="wp-block-list"><li>D.J. Augustin. The 33-year-old point guard played in 37 games for the Bucks after the team signed him in the preceding offseason. He struggled, below his career averages in shooting and scoring, and was packaged in a deal to Houston for P.J. Tucker before the trade deadline.</li><li>Torrey Craig. Interestingly, the Bucks wound up facing Craig in the NBA Finals, since he&#8217;d by then become a member of the Suns. Signed before the season as a free agent, the defensive stopper was traded in March to the Suns for cash. Unable to get meaningful rotation minutes, he played only 18 games for the Bucks; he played in 22 playoff games alone for the Suns and averaged 4.0 points per game in the postseason.</li><li>D.J. Wilson. The Bucks made Wilson the 17th pick in 2017, and despite flashes, he never developed into a starter. Milwaukee sent him to Houston midseason in the deal that brought back Tucker. They literally traded two DJs for a PJ.</li><li>Rodions Kurucs. He came back to the Bucks from the Rockets in the same deal that brought Tucker over, but he played only five games for Milwaukee before the team waived him.</li><li>Jaylen Adams. The point guard was on a two-way contract signed in November 2020, but he was waived in March. He played in seven games with the Bucks and hasn&#8217;t appeared in the NBA since.</li></ul><p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Milwaukee Bucks coaching staff</strong><p class="wp-block-paragraph">Mike Budenholzer, head coach. Budenholzer never missed the playoffs in five seasons at the helm, going 271-120 in the regular season. He later became coach of the Suns in 2024-25 but was fired from that post after one season.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Assistant coaches Vin Baker, Chad Forcier, Darvin Ham, Ben Sullivan, Charles Lee, Pat St. Andrews, trainer Scott Faust. Ham became coach of the Los Angeles Lakers for two seasons and is now back in Milwaukee as an assistant. Lee will enter his third year as head coach of Charlotte in 2026-27.<p class="wp-block-paragraph">This article originally appeared on Milwaukee Journal Sentinel: Nobody is left from the 2021 Bucks title team; here’s where they went<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Reporting by JR Radcliffe, Milwaukee Journal Sentinel / Milwaukee Journal Sentinel<p class="wp-block-paragraph">USA TODAY Network via Reuters Connect<aside><div
